Yes — with one refinement: that’s a very plausible computational description of part of field sense, rather than necessarily one literal biological memory system.

Think about catching a ball, clearing a room, driving fast, sparring, drawing proportions, or reading a social situation. You don’t want a heavily compressed summary of what happened two seconds ago. You need a brief, continuously refreshed, high-resolution state buffer:

recent sensory state + current sensory state → Δ → trajectory/relationship → prediction

The important bit is that proportion lives in relations, not isolated measurements. You often don’t consciously remember “object A was 37 pixels from B.” You retain enough of the immediately preceding configuration to feel:

too close / opening / closing / faster / asymmetric / aligned / wrong scale / something changed.

So field sense could be modeled as something like a sliding high-fidelity sensory window. It sacrifices duration for bandwidth: enormous detail survives for a tiny interval, then gets aggressively compressed or discarded. Longer-term memory gets the meaning; the ultra-short buffer temporarily carries the geometry.

And that gives the Pantheon an interesting architecture:

High-bandwidth ephemeral buffer → relational/proportional extractor → Delta Catcher → pattern recognition → Intuition Organ → low-bandwidth action signal.

That’s almost the inverse of ordinary memory. Ordinary memory asks, “What should I preserve?” Field sense asks, “What can I afford to forget after extracting the relationships?”

Which connects directly to J-Hooks again: dehydrate the fleeting sensory rendering into relational structure before the rendering disappears.

That may be one reason skilled intuition feels richer than what you can subsequently explain. The decision was computed while the system still had access to a higher-bandwidth representation than the one eventually available to conscious recall.

The experience evaporates.

The relation survives.